// The revl stdlib SERVER binding module (roadmap item 457 S3,
// docs/design/457-endpoint-one-definition.md).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- what this is
// `stdlib/http.rvl` (item 456) is the typed HTTP CONTRACT — `Request`,
// `Response`, `Outcome`, `Method`, `ApiError` — the data a routed operation
// speaks. This module is the other half item 457 S3 needs: the declaration of
// the HOST-PROVIDED server itself, the Cordis `ctx.server` a `host` row binds so
// a routed service is actually SERVED on the ts tier.
//
//     composition Notes {
//       use "stdlib/server.rvl"
//       host @server provides server: Server   // Cordis ctx.server, ts tier
//       ...
//     }
//
// A `host` row's provider is synthesized from THIS service declaration plus the
// shipped host shim (`backends/typescript/revl_server_ts.ts` over
// `@cordisjs/server`), reached through the reviewed `@ts ref` door — never a
// `globalThis` bridge (design note 530, answered by the row table). See
// `src/revl/synthesize.py` `_host_source` and `HOST_SHIMS`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the Server surface
// The method set mirrors `@cordisjs/server`'s `Server.methods` EXACTLY —
// `['get', 'post', 'put', 'patch', 'delete', 'head']` (packages/core/src/index.ts,
// minus the `all` wildcard and the `ws` upgrade, as `stdlib/http.rvl`'s `Method`
// variant already does) — so the route table a `route` clause lands (item 457
// S1) and the host server it registers on cannot disagree on the verb set.
//
// Each verb is the REGISTRATION primitive for one path: `ctx.server.get(path,
// handler)`. The HANDLER argument is NOT passed from revl here — it is
// synthesized and bound by the ts route-registration EMITTER (item 457 S3's
// emitted-`ctx.server` half, docs/design/457 §"The five derived artifacts" (1)),
// which decodes the typed `Request`, binds the operation's parameters per the
// route bind table, calls the provide method and encodes the `Outcome`. So the
// revl-facing method carries the path it registers; the handler is the compiler's
// to wire. The exact handler-value contract is finalized against the exemplary
// app (item 462); this declaration pins the verb set and the emission bound the
// binding rests on.
//
// Every method is `emission`: reaching the host runtime to register a route is a
// boundary crossing (it mutates the server's route table), so a plain `fn` would
// be refused as unhostable — G4 read at the host boundary (item 457 S3, the same
// rule `remote` reads at the client, 424 D-424c.2).
//
// Each method DECLARES that boundary rather than spelling `emission` bare
// (issue #2105, taking docs/design/561-undeclared-emission-boundary.md's
// composition half). The token names the host operation — the export the
// shipped shim `backends/typescript/revl_server_ts.ts` binds, one per verb,
// enumerated in `synthesize.HOST_SHIMS["Server"]["caps"]` — which is the SAME
// crossing for every `host` row: the row's label names the WIRING (the extern
// name, the key, the isolate realm), never the boundary, so one fixed token per
// verb is true for every composition. `synthesize._host_source` spells that
// token as the synthesized extern's declared scope, and `check_hostable` refuses
// a service whose declaration names anything else, so the claim is checked
// against the shim rather than believed. Bare, this service's reach was the
// unnameable boundary, and a component that wired `server: Server` could not be
// told apart from one that reached the kernel (issue #1223's rule, read at the
// fold). What is still conflated is two `host` rows behind one service name —
// the residual §561:184 records as its own item, untouched here.

// The host HTTP server (`@cordisjs/server`'s `ctx.server`). One registration
// method per verb `stdlib/http.rvl`'s `Method` variant admits.
service Server {
  emission[host.server.get] fn get(path: Str) -> Unit
  emission[host.server.post] fn post(path: Str) -> Unit
  emission[host.server.put] fn put(path: Str) -> Unit
  emission[host.server.patch] fn patch(path: Str) -> Unit
  emission[host.server.delete] fn delete(path: Str) -> Unit
  emission[host.server.head] fn head(path: Str) -> Unit
}
